(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-lipid-fibrates-niacin-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Fibrates, Niacin & Lipid Profiles Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What mediates the cutaneous flushing caused by niacin?","choices":["Bradykinin","Prostaglandins","Histamine","Serotonin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Niacin flushing is a prostaglandin-mediated effect, which is why premedication with aspirin minimizes it. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient with high triglycerides has a history of symptomatic gout. Which triglyceride-lowering option is best avoided?","choices":["Niacin","Fenofibrate","Gemfibrozil","Bezafibrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises uric acid, so symptomatic gout and significant hyperuricemia are relative contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which organ dysfunction, when severe, contraindicates a fibrate?","choices":["Pulmonary or cardiac","Thyroid or adrenal","Hepatic or renal","Pancreatic or splenic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe hepatic or renal dysfunction contraindicates fibrates, along with pregnancy and existing gallbladder disease. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is ezetimibe's effect on HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["A large rise","A sharp fall","A rise greater than niacin","Insignificant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ezetimibe lowers LDL (low-density lipoprotein) modestly and triglycerides slightly, with an insignificant effect on HDL. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Why is niacin a relative contraindication in a patient with symptomatic gout?","choices":["It causes gallstones","It raises uric acid","It raises potassium","It lowers kidney blood flow"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At larger doses niacin raises liver enzymes, glucose and uric acid, so gout and significant hyperuricemia are relative contraindications. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A patient on warfarin is started on gemfibrozil. Which interaction is expected?","choices":["Reduced warfarin absorption","Reduced anticoagulant effect","No interaction is expected","Increased anticoagulant effect"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates increase the anticoagulant effect of warfarin; they also interact with statins, ezetimibe and bile acid sequestrants. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which adverse effects are listed for the lovastatin and extended-release niacin combination?","choices":["Cough, angioedema and hyperkalemia","Bloating, constipation and flatulence","Gallstones, nausea and diarrhea","Hepatotoxicity, myopathy and flushing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The combination carries the statin's muscle and liver risks and niacin's flushing. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why is a statin combined with a fibrate only with caution?","choices":["Higher risk of myopathy","Loss of LDL lowering","Higher risk of flushing","Higher risk of hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both classes can cause myopathy, so they are combined cautiously; gemfibrozil is a relative contraindication with statins. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which two classes both lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein) substantially?","choices":["Resins and ezetimibe","Fibrates and niacin","Statins and resins","Ezetimibe and statins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both lower triglycerides by 20 to 50% and raise HDL (niacin 15 to 35%, fibrates 10 to 35%). — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which class produces the largest rise in HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["Nicotinic acid","Ezetimibe","Bile acid sequestrants","Statins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises HDL by 15 to 35%, the largest rise of any class; fibrates follow at 10 to 35%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How do fibrates raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)?","choices":["They block HDL breakdown in the gut","They inhibit HMG-CoA reductase","They increase ApoA-1 expression","They bind cholesterol in the bile"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. PPAR-alpha activation increases expression of ApoA-1, the HDL apolipoprotein, raising HDL by 10 to 35%. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient starting niacin asks how to reduce the flushing. What is recommended?","choices":["Take aspirin beforehand","Take it with alcohol","Take it with grapefruit juice","Take it with an antacid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Flushing is prostaglandin mediated and is minimized by premedication with aspirin. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which lifestyle measure raises HDL (high-density lipoprotein) cholesterol?","choices":["Grapefruit juice","Salt substitutes","Exercise","Fasting before meals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. HDL increases with exercise, a lifestyle measure that complements the drug classes that raise it, niacin and fibrates. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which form of niacin is NOT effective as an antilipemic?","choices":["Nicotinic acid","Niacinamide","Extended-release niacin","Immediate-release niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Niacin is a B-complex vitamin; nicotinic acid is the antilipemic form, and its amide, niacinamide (nicotinamide), is not effective. — Lipids.pptx, Slide 50"}]};
